<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleisthenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 570 – after 508 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonidae</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cleisthenes was an Athenian statesman of the Alcmaeonid family, regarded as the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy">Athenian democracy</a>. After the fall of the tyrant Hippias, he reorganised the citizen body in 508–507 BCE into ten new tribes based on local demes, broke the power of the old aristocratic clans and created the Council of Five Hundred. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostracism">Ostracism</a> is also traditionally credited to him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Cleisthenes</b>' reforms of 508/507 BCE, after the fall of the tyrant Hippias, created the democracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the tyrant Hippias was expelled in 510 BCE, the aristocrats Isagoras and <b>Cleisthenes</b> contended for power.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After Harmodius and Aristogeiton killed Hipparchus in 514 BCE, Hippias grew harsher, and in 510 BCE a Spartan army drove him out; the reforms of <b>Cleisthenes</b> followed in 508/507 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The first well-documented direct democracy was that of Athens, from the reforms of <b>Cleisthenes</b> in 508–507 BCE until 322 BCE, when the Macedonian regent Antipater limited citizenship to men owning at least 2,000 drachmas.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>From the reforms of <b>Cleisthenes</b> in 508 BCE the Assembly of all adult male citizens governed the city, so its ladder begins not with a ruler but with the richest citizens.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-530" aria-label="Classical Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cleisthenes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-530">What was here in 530 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
